lesson: 155
title: Difference and multiple: how many times as many, and how many more
level: primary-3
stars: 1
explain: If the bigger amount is 3 times the smaller, draw the smaller as 1 unit and the bigger as 3 units.
explain: The difference is then 3 - 1 = 2 units. If the difference is 24, one unit is 24 ÷ 2 = 12.
explain: So the smaller amount is 12 and the bigger is 3 × 12 = 36. Check: 36 - 12 = 24.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing s=3..40 k=2..6
let: d = s * (k - 1)
need: s * k <= 200
answer: s * k
exercise: {a} has {k.words} times as many {t} as {b}. {a} has {d} more {t} than {b}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: A red box holds {k.words} times as many {t} as a blue box. The red box holds {d} more {t} than the blue one. How many {t} are in the red box?
exercise: In an orchard there are {k.words} times as many apple trees as pear trees. There are {d} more apple trees than pear trees. How many apple trees are there?
exercise: A rope is {k.words} times as long as a string, and it is {d} cm longer. How long is the rope in centimetres?
exercise: On Monday a shop sold {k.words} times as many pens as on Tuesday, that is, {d} pens more. How many pens did it sell on Monday?
test: {b} has {d} fewer {t} than {a}, and {a} has {k.words} times as many {t} as {b}. How many {t} has {a} got?
test: The bigger pile has {k.words} times as many {t} as the smaller pile, and the difference between the piles is {d} {t}. How many {t} are in the bigger pile?
test: A big bucket holds {d} litres more water than a small bucket, and {k.words} times as much. How many litres does the big bucket hold?
test: How many pages did {a} read on Sunday, if it was {d} pages more than on Saturday and {k.words} times as many?
exercise: {a} has {d} more {t} than {b}, and {k.words} times as many. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: A shop in Ohio sold {k} times as many pies on Saturday as on Friday. On Saturday it sold {d} more pies. How many pies did it sell on Saturday?
exercise: How many {t} does {a} have, if {a} has {k} times as many {t} as {b} and {b} has {d} fewer?
exercise: A green jar holds {k.words} times as many {t} as a yellow jar, and the yellow jar holds {d} fewer {t}. How many {t} are in the green jar?
exercise: A town school in Manchester has {k} times as many pupils as a village school, and {d} pupils more. How many pupils does the town school have?
exercise: A tower in a park in Toronto is {k.words} times as tall as a house and {d} m taller. How tall is the tower in metres?
exercise: There are {k} times as many frogs as ducks in a pond in Ireland, so there are {d} more frogs than ducks. How many frogs are there?
exercise: A long train in Australia has {k} times as many carriages as a short one, that is, {d} carriages more. How many carriages has the long train?
test: {b} has not as many {t} as {a}: {d} fewer. {a} has {k.words} times as many as {b}. How many {t} does {a} have?
test: A big school bus in Texas carries {k} times as many children as a minibus, which is {d} more children. How many children does the big bus carry?
test: The difference between two numbers is {d}, and the larger is {k} times the smaller. What is the larger number?
test: An old plum tree in Kent gave {k.words} times as many plums as a young tree, {d} plums more. How many plums did the old tree give?
test: {a} ran {k} times as far as {b}, which was {d} m further. How many metres did {a} run?
test: How many stamps are in {a}'s album, if it has {d} stamps more than {b}'s and {k.words} times as many?
exercise: {a} has {k} times as many {t} as {b}, and {d} more. how many {a} got
exercise: red box {k} times the blue one, {d} {t} more too. how many in the red
exercise: my mate says it's just {d}, the more bit. nah. {a}'s got {k} times {b}'s {t}, {d} more than {b}. how many {a}
exercise: {a} got {d+3} — no wait, {d} more {t} than {b}, and {k} times as many. {a}'s total?
test: {b} has {d} less than {a}, {a} has {k} times {b}'s. how many {t} {a} got then
test: {a}'s {t}: {k} times {b}'s and {d} more, and {a}'s birthday's in {k+2} days lol. how many has {a}
test: {b} says {a} has {s}. nah that's {b}'s own lot. {a} has {k} times as many {t} as {b}, {d} more — {a}'s got?
exam: It is given that {a} possesses {k.words} times as many {t} as {b}, and that the holding of {a} exceeds that of {b} by {d}. Determine the number of {t} in the possession of {a}.
exam: Whereas the contents of a red box are {k} times those of a blue box, and the red box contains {d} {t} more than the blue box, ascertain the number of {t} contained in the red box.
exam: Ascertain the larger of two numbers, given that the larger is {k.words} times the smaller and exceeds it by {d}.
exam: Two quantities of {t} are such that the first is {k} times the second and the difference between them is {d}. State the first quantity.
exercise: {a} was economical with the truth and said {a.he} and {b} got the same from Gran. Really {a} got {k.words} times as many dollars as {b}, which is {d} dollars more. How many dollars did {a} get?
exercise: When Grandpa passed away, he left {a} {k.words} times as many stamps as {b}, which was {d} more stamps than {b} got. How many stamps did {a} get?
test: {b}'s telling porkies again, swears they got the same. rubbish. {a} has {k} times as many {t} as {b}, {d} more. how many has {a} got then, eh
